From: Codex <codex@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] ieframe, dsrole, samcli, ntshrui: what Windows 7's folder windows need

Purpose: show Windows 7's folder windows (Win+E, Computer).
Windows 7 behavior: its shdocvw.dll keeps shdocvw's ordinals and forwards
most of them to ieframe.dll with the same ordinal, among them 158 and 159
(SHRestricted2A and SHRestricted2W), which explorerframe calls while it
builds a folder window. Its shell32.dll delay-loads dsrole.dll
(DsRoleGetPrimaryDomainInformation and DsRoleFreeMemory, for the
workgroup shown in the details pane), samcli.dll (the Net* user and group
functions that netapi32 also exports) and ntshrui.dll (IsPathSharedW,
GetNetResourceFromLocalPathW and GetLocalPathFromNetResourceW, which
linkinfo.dll delay-loads too).
Wine before: ieframe had no ordinals 158 and 159; Wine had no dsrole,
samcli and ntshrui. Each call ended in Wine's delay load failure stub, and
the shell then called a null pointer.
Change: ieframe exports 158 and 159 as SHRestricted2A and SHRestricted2W,
which return 0 (no restriction), as Wine's shdocvw ones do. New builtin
dsrole.dll and samcli.dll forward to netapi32's functions. New builtin
ntshrui.dll answers that no path is shared and no network resource names
a local path (Wine shares no folders); its class factory has no sharing
pages. configure and configure.ac add the three libraries.
Evidence: Windows 7 SP1 x64 shdocvw.dll, shell32.dll and linkinfo.dll
import and export tables. With this change Win+E shows the Computer
folder with its navigation pane, drives and details pane (workgroup,
memory and processor).
Scope: new exports and new libraries; programs that did not find them
before now get these answers.
